WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.298
SQL injection is a risk created when untrusted data is treated as part of
a database command.

2
00:00:05.298 --> 00:00:10.114
The defensive concern is not limited to one framework, one route, or one
login form.

3
00:00:10.114 --> 00:00:20.434
Python applications use databases from Flask views, Django models, FastAPI
services, scripts, internal tools, data pipelines, scheduled jobs, APIs,
and background workers.

4
00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:24.493
Any place that builds a query from data can become a security boundary.

5
00:00:24.493 --> 00:00:26.557
It helps to separate related ideas.

6
00:00:26.557 --> 00:00:32.198
Input validation checks whether data has the expected type, size, format,
and business meaning.

7
00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:37.358
Query construction decides how the application turns a request into a
database command.

8
00:00:37.358 --> 00:00:42.106
Authorization decides whether the caller may perform the action on the
resource.

9
00:00:42.106 --> 00:00:45.890
Database permissions limit what the application account can do.

10
00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:52.494
SQL injection prevention focuses first on query construction: keeping data
values separate from SQL structure.

11
00:00:52.494 --> 00:00:57.517
The impact can touch confidentiality, integrity, availability, and
business logic.

12
00:00:57.517 --> 00:01:10.520
Unsafe queries may expose data the application did not intend to return,
alter records incorrectly, break workflow assumptions, create operational
instability, or bypass business rules implemented in application code.

13
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:17.538
Even an internal reporting tool can become high impact if it has broad
database access or handles sensitive information.

14
00:01:17.538 --> 00:01:20.565
Risk follows capability, not just public exposure.

15
00:01:20.565 --> 00:01:24.968
Prevention begins with how queries are built, not only with how inputs
look.

16
00:01:24.968 --> 00:01:35.494
The safest default is to use parameterized queries, safe ORM patterns,
allowlisted dynamic structure, and reviewed data-access helpers
consistently across every database path.

17
00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:44.094
A team should not have one safe pattern for normal routes and a separate
unsafe shortcut for admin screens, reports, migrations, or background
jobs.

18
00:01:44.094 --> 00:01:48.360
Consistency is what turns a secure coding rule into an engineering habit.
